/P 67 0 R
/T (1. Executive Summary Low-code and no-code \(LCNC\) development has moved from a departmental productivity shortcut to a core pillar of enterprise software strategy. Industry analysts now estimate that roughly 70 to 75 percent of new enterprise applications will be built using low-code or no-code technologies, with the global market value climbing well past the sixty-billion-dollar mark and continuing to expand at a compound annual growth rate above 25 percent. This shift is not incremental; it represents a structural change in who builds software, how quickly it is built, and what governance is required to keep it safe.)
/E (1. Executive Summary Low-code and no-code \(LCNC\) development has moved from a departmental productivity shortcut to a core pillar of enterprise software strategy. Industry analysts now estimate that roughly 70 to 75 percent of new enterprise applications will be built using low-code or no-code technologies, with the global market value climbing well past the sixty-billion-dollar mark and continuing to expand at a compound annual growth rate above 25 percent. This shift is not incremental; it represents a structural change in who builds software, how quickly it is built, and what governance is required to keep it safe.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
101 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H4
/P 67 0 R
/T (Three forces are converging to accelerate this transformation. First, a persistent shortage of professional developers has pushed organizations to empower citizen developers, business analysts, and operations staff to build their own tools. Second, generative AI has fused with visual development environments, allowing applications and even autonomous agents to be described in plain language and generated automatically. Third, enterprises are formalizing governance frameworks, security controls, and platform consolidation strategies to manage the risk that comes with democratized development at scale.)
/E (Three forces are converging to accelerate this transformation. First, a persistent shortage of professional developers has pushed organizations to empower citizen developers, business analysts, and operations staff to build their own tools. Second, generative AI has fused with visual development environments, allowing applications and even autonomous agents to be described in plain language and generated automatically. Third, enterprises are formalizing governance frameworks, security controls, and platform consolidation strategies to manage the risk that comes with democratized development at scale.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
